I have such a great recommendation for  you today not long ago my friend Charlie  gave me a copy of the book the lessons  of history by will and Ariel Durant will  and Ariel Durant just their biography is  interesting go on Wikipedia and read a  little bit about them they have a book  called the lessons of history and the  only way I can describe it is it's an  incredibly concise summary of culture  and civilization of a mankind okay and  it's a dozen or so pretty short essays  two to four pages each the books only  about a hundred and ten pages okay it is  written so well it condenses such  complex ideas based on hundreds or even  many more years of survey and  understanding of humanity into a couple  of different pages it looks at war it  looks at racism it looks at the history  of history I could not possibly  recommend this book more highly because  of the way in which it provides  perspective about so many of the broader  issues that continue to resurface for  humans and you know one effort I'm  making in terms of what I read Pat is  I'm thinking when I we have a limited  amount of time to read write well for it  for anything in life but reading is one  of those things that you're only gonna  read a certain number of books and I'm  trying to ask myself the question is the  subject matter of the book something  that is only relevant based on a fad or  trend right now or is this a book that  is going to be more broadly informative  because it relates to areas of of the  world areas of life that are either  recurring or ever-present and if by that  standard this book should be really  close to the top of the list for  everybody right because everyone always  talks about history repeating itself or  so for example Napoleon tried to invade  Russia failed Hitler tried to invade  Russia also failed so these things keep  happening and take on different forms  and now with the the rise of Donald  Trump and his authoritarian rhetoric yes  we can we can look back even just a  hundred year  ago or less and draw comparisons to  leaders of other authoritarian regime  and the other thing that the book  actually a lot of it applies to what's  going on right now there's one chapter I  forget which chapter or essay it is and  it talks about how fundamentally freedom  and equality are opposed forces maybe  not in the literal sense but if you have  total freedom you're not going to have  equality because total freedom  encompasses the ability of some humans  to oppress others and for wealth to  concentrate significantly so total  freedom is the opposite of total  equality total equality can only be  achieved if you go in a more  authoritarian direction based on the  lessons of history as the book is called  and it really lays out these concepts in  a very very good way and where I end up  after reading a book like this it's  thinking that on most issues of  paramount importance to society and  culture extremist solutions are not  sustainable long term and the book in  talking about some of the cycles that we  see explains how waves of  authoritarianism lead to backlashes that  lead to less authoritarianism which  leads to growth in inequality which  leads to a different type of backlash  and the cycle sort of repeats itself so  in any case yeah this is just one  example of what's in the book the book  is the lessons of history by will and  Ariel Durant you can find this and all  of my recommendations at david  pakman.com slash recommendations
 

